  en: {
    title: 'sA · sV · sonic analysis',
    sub: 'Essentia.js (WASM) · WebAudio API · OffscreenCanvas · Web Workers',
    intro: 'Acoustic analysis runs entirely in the client. sA extracts descriptors; sV recomposes them as a shared temporal surface for investigating form, energy, gesture, timbre, editing and perceptual correlation. The visualization does not replace listening: it creates an operational plane where ear, measurement, memory and decision can work on the same trace.',
    refsHeading: 'references',
    sections: [
      {
        heading: 'sA descriptors',
        items: [
          {
            title: 'Pitch · f0 with YIN',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Pitch estimates the fundamental frequency, the dominant period that lets a sound behave as a note, stable voice or tonal center. YIN reduces octave errors by comparing the signal with delayed versions of itself and looking for normalized difference minima.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'In nature it can trace vocal inflection, bird song or periodic mechanical vibration. In music production it detects vibrato, glissandi, out-of-tune doubles, pitch-correction candidates and points where a noisy texture becomes melodic.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'YIN computes <code>d(&tau;) = &sum;<sub>j</sub>(x<sub>j</sub> - x<sub>j+&tau;</sub>)<sup>2</sup></code> and the cumulative mean normalized difference <code>d\'(&tau;) = d(&tau;) / ((1/&tau;) &sum;<sub>j=1..&tau;</sub>d(j))</code>. Final pitch is <code>f0 = sampleRate / &tau;</code> at the first reliable minimum.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'dBFS · instant digital energy',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'dBFS measures amplitude relative to the digital maximum: 0 dBFS is the ceiling before clipping. It describes headroom and digital occupancy, not full perceived loudness.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It is a gain-staging tool for recording, editing, mixing and mastering. A snare peak at -1 dBFS can feel quieter than a limited pad at -8 dBFS because duration, spectrum and density shape perception.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'For a window of <code>N</code> samples, <code>rms = sqrt((1/N) &sum; x[n]<sup>2</sup>)</code> and <code>dBFS = 20 log10(rms / ref)</code>, with <code>ref = 1.0</code> in normalized floating audio. Digital peak uses <code>20 log10(max(|x[n]|))</code>.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'LUFS M/S/I · perceptual loudness',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'LUFS approximates perceived loudness through K-weighting and temporal windows. Momentary tracks microdynamics, Short-term stabilizes phrases, and Integrated summarizes the whole programme with gates that ignore very quiet material.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'For released music, streaming, broadcast, podcast and film, LUFS explains why files with similar peaks may feel radically different. It helps compare masters, expose over-compression and avoid violent level jumps.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'ITU-R BS.1770 uses K-filtering and weighted multichannel summation: <code>LKFS = -0.691 + 10 log10(&sum; G<sub>i</sub> z<sub>i</sub>)</code>. Momentary commonly uses 400 ms; Short-term 3 s; Integrated applies absolute and relative gating.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Spectral centroid · brightness',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Spectral centroid is the frequency center of mass. When it rises, sound tends to feel brighter, sharper or more forward; when it falls, darker, duller or heavier.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It separates water, wind, dry leaves or struck wood by high-frequency distribution. In production it supports EQ decisions for dull vocals, aggressive cymbals, harsh guitars, filter openings, piano attacks and pick noise.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'With spectral magnitudes <code>|X(k)|</code> and frequencies <code>f<sub>k</sub></code>, <code>C = (&sum; f<sub>k</sub>|X(k)|) / (&sum; |X(k)|)</code>. It is usually computed per STFT frame and optionally smoothed into a temporal brightness curve.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Spectral spread · timbral width',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Spread measures how far energy disperses around the centroid. Two sounds can share the same average brightness while one is compact and tonal and the other broad, noisy or saturated.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'A stable flute concentrates energy; a distorted chord or granular texture distributes it. In mixing it helps identify layers occupying too much spectrum and compare pads, choirs, percussion and tone-noise hybrids.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'It is spectral standard deviation: <code>spread = sqrt((&sum;(f<sub>k</sub>-C)<sup>2</sup>|X(k)|) / (&sum;|X(k)|))</code>. High spread means high variance around the centroid, not necessarily higher loudness.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Spectral skewness · mass asymmetry',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Skewness describes asymmetry in the spectral distribution. It reveals whether mass is biased toward lows with a high-frequency tail or toward highs with another concentration shape.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'In production it flags basses with useful harmonics, nasal vocals, excessive hi-hats, shifted resonances or unbalanced instruments. It differentiates a kick, scraped string and fricative consonant through spectral geometry.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'As a normalized central moment: <code>skew = &mu;<sub>3</sub> / &sigma;<sup>3</sup></code>, where <code>&mu;<sub>3</sub> = E[(f-C)<sup>3</sup>]</code> and <code>&sigma;</code> is spread. Sign and magnitude depend on magnitude/power weighting.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Spectral kurtosis · peaks, tails and roughness',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Kurtosis measures how peaked or flat a spectral distribution is relative to a reference shape. A spectrum dominated by clear partials differs from broad noise or a snare-wire texture.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It helps locate resonances, whistles, painful peaks, de-essing targets, surgical EQ areas and places where saturation or friction breaks partial clarity. In sound design it controls the transition from tone to noise.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: '<code>kurt = &mu;<sub>4</sub> / &sigma;<sup>4</sup></code>, with <code>&mu;<sub>4</sub> = E[(f-C)<sup>4</sup>]</code>. Some implementations report excess kurtosis by subtracting 3; comparative reading across regions is usually more robust.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Spectral slope · energy falloff',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Slope approximates the global spectral tilt: how quickly energy falls or rises across frequency. It summarizes timbral balance more structurally than a simple treble control.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'A steep negative slope can feel warm, veiled or distant; a flatter slope can feel present or abrasive. In studio work it informs microphone distance, take darkness, archival age and filter sweeps.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'It is obtained by linear regression over <code>(f<sub>k</sub>, |X(k)|)</code> or magnitude in dB: <code>slope = cov(f, A) / var(f)</code>. The result depends on linear/log scale and selected bin range.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Spectral flux · attacks and motion',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Flux measures spectral change between consecutive frames. It is sensitive to attacks, articulations, edits and internal transformations, especially when new energy appears.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It reveals piano strikes, consonants, pizzicati, kicks, glitches, edit cuts, texture shifts and beat-slicing material. In natural sound it separates continuous rain from isolated drops or steady fire from crackles.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'A common form uses positive half-wave rectification: <code>flux(t)=&sum;<sub>k</sub> max(0, |X<sub>t</sub>(k)|-|X<sub>t-1</sub>(k)|)<sup>2</sup></code>. Normalizing spectra separates shape change from global level change.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'T1/T2/T3 · tristimulus and harmonic color',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'Tristimulus distributes harmonic energy into fundamental, early harmonics and upper harmonics. It condenses tonal color into three functional zones: base, identity and brightness/complexity.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'High T1 can feel pure or hollow; T2 gives instrumental body; T3 adds edge, air or richness. It is useful for comparing basses, strings, brass, voices, additive synthesis and orchestration.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'With harmonic amplitudes <code>A<sub>h</sub></code>: <code>T1=A1/&sum;A<sub>h</sub></code>, <code>T2=(A2+A3+A4)/&sum;A<sub>h</sub></code>, <code>T3=(&sum;<sub>h&gt;4</sub>A<sub>h</sub>)/&sum;A<sub>h</sub></code>. It requires usable f0 and harmonic tracking.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'HNR · harmonic-to-noise ratio',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'HNR compares periodic energy against aperiodic energy. High values suggest stable periodicity; low values indicate breath, friction, scraping, distortion, turbulence or noise floor.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'In voice it separates clean singing, whisper, hoarseness and air. In production it evaluates vocal takes, saturation, breaths, room noise, acoustic instrument texture and the continuum between note and noisy matter.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'It may be written as <code>HNR = 10 log10(E<sub>harm</sub>/E<sub>noise</sub>)</code>. Autocorrelation approximations can use normalized peak <code>r</code>: <code>HNR = 10 log10(r/(1-r))</code>, with caution for non-stationary signals.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'ZCR · zero-crossing rate',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'ZCR counts sign changes in the waveform. It is simple but reveals internal speed: high or noisy signals cross zero more often than smooth low-frequency tones.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It detects fricatives, hi-hats, clicks, glitches, tape noise, bright percussion or differences between sub-bass and fine noise. Sounds with equal RMS can have very different ZCR and feel unrelated.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: '<code>ZCR = (1/(2N)) &sum;<sub>n=1..N</sub> |sgn(x[n]) - sgn(x[n-1])|</code>. It is sensitive to near-zero noise, DC offset and filtering; short windows and minimum thresholds improve stability.' },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        heading: 'sV layers',
        items: [
          {
            title: 'SPEC · spectrogram',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'SPEC projects energy over frequency and time. It is a time-frequency cartography: horizontal events, bands, attacks, tails, resonances and noise become inspectable shapes.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'In production it reveals sibilance, sub-bass, resonances, cuts, reverb, hum, mix holes and transients. It compares voice, drum, drone or granular texture without reducing them to pitch notation.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'It is based on STFT: <code>X(m,k)=&sum;<sub>n</sub>x[n]w[n-mH]e<sup>-j2&pi;kn/N</sup></code>. The image often displays <code>20 log10(|X(m,k)|)</code>; window and hop sizes control time/frequency resolution.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'WAV · waveform',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'WAV shows amplitude over time. It is the most direct surface for editing starts, silences, transients, fades, visible clipping and macro-dynamic structure.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'Studio workflows use it to clean takes, align drums, cut voice, sync material, detect displaced hits and compare envelopes. Natural sounds show pulses, breathing, footsteps, waves or impacts.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'The curve is <code>x[n]</code> or a block envelope: <code>peak=max(|x[n]|)</code> and <code>rms=sqrt(mean(x[n]<sup>2</sup>))</code>. Waveform alone does not encode full timbre; many spectra can share similar amplitude shapes.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'ENR · energy',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'ENR summarizes frame-by-frame energy. It reveals dynamics, formal breathing, accumulation, rest, event density and intensity changes without inspecting every sample.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'In a track it shows builds, drops, breaks, accents and resting areas. In production it guides compression, automation, silence editing, section balance and comparison between dynamic intention and measured result.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'Frame energy can be <code>E(t)=&sum;<sub>n=0..N-1</sub>x<sub>t</sub>[n]<sup>2</sup></code> or <code>RMS(t)=sqrt(E(t)/N)</code>. Exponential smoothing or overlapped windows keep isolated peaks from dominating the reading.' },
            ],
          },
          {
