              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'MOT represents change rather than state. A sustained note may have high energy and little motion; a granular texture may have moderate energy and intense motion.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It locates transients, glitches, edits, obvious sidechain, rhythmic variation, turbulence and regions where material transforms. It separates stable wind from breaking branches or continuous water from irregular drops.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'It can derive from flux, feature-vector distance or difference norms: <code>M(t)=||F(t)-F(t-1)||</code>. If <code>F</code> combines centroid, spread, energy and flatness, MOT captures multivariate timbral motion.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'GRV · harmonic gravity',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'GRV is a Musiki-derived descriptor: it models perceptual weight toward dense, low or stabilizing regions. It is not a physical law; it is an operational reading of sonic mass.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It helps detect when bass dominates, when an arrangement lacks foundation or when a cinematic impact accumulates body. In sound design it compares drones, risers, hits, sublayers and textures that sustain or crush the field.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'One formulation combines band energy with inverse-frequency weights: <code>GRV(t)=(&sum;E<sub>k,t</sub> w<sub>k</sub>)/(&sum;E<sub>k,t</sub>)</code>, with <code>w<sub>k</sub>=1/log2(2+f<sub>k</sub>/f0)</code>. Interpret it relative to corpus calibration.' },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'TEN · tension / flatness',
            steps: [
              { label: '1. Synopsis', body: 'TEN uses spectral flatness as an index of timbral tension: how noise-like a spectrum is compared with clear tonal or partial organization.' },
              { label: '2. Example', body: 'It controls saturation, noise floor, synth roughness, cymbals, ambience, distortion and threatening layers. It describes tension without relying on tonal harmony: friction, air and noise also create directionality.' },
              { label: '3. Hard data', body: 'Flatness is geometric mean divided by arithmetic mean: <code>flatness = exp(mean(log(|X(k)|+&epsilon;))) / mean(|X(k)|+&epsilon;)</code>. Values near 1 suggest white-noise-like spectra; low values indicate tonal peaks.' },
